WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.520
They're everywhere.

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:06.080
Apparently algorithms are in the
social media apps we use every day,

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.280
in search engines,
even in dating apps.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:09.880
But I'll be honest -

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.720
I've got absolutely no idea
what they actually are

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.280
or how they work or who makes them.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:16.960
Should we be worried about them?

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.680
Can they think for themselves?

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.720
Rather than be baffled by science,
I'm going to go find out.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:33.520
Where does every good piece of
research start? A search engine.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.040
So what is an algorithm?

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.400
Can I even spell it?

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.000
Oh, I think so. OK.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:47.480
A process or set of rules
to be followed in calculations

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:52.400
or other problem solving operations,
especially by a computer.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:54.680
Sounds like a riddle.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.840
After an hour of searching
about the internet

00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:59.840
it all felt a little overwhelming.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.720
So many different definitions without
any clarity in what they actually do.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:08.320
During my search I came across
professor Victoria Nash

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.400
from the Oxford Internet Institute.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.440
I called to pick her brains.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.720
Speaking to Victoria has helped me
understand what algorithms are

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:47.680
but how can the same thing
that helps me bake a cake,

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.480
also give me the best results
from a search engine?

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:52.800
No idea.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:55.520
I'm taking a trip to Oxford

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.960
to visit one of Victoria's
colleagues, Dr Bernie Hogan.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:00.640
Bernie, great to see you.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:01.920
Jon, nice to see you.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.680
Where on earth are we?

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:06.080
So this is our
university's data centre.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.200
You know, it's pretty big.
It's really noisy.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:10.560
Really noisy.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:12.760
There's a lot of computation
happening here.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.640
Each one of these belong
to different departments.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.240
They're doing different
kinds of calculations.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.320
So there's thousands of algorithms

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:22.480
going on in all these
massive boxes like now?

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:24.440
Thousands? Try billions.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:25.520
Billions?

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:26.760
Billions of algorithms.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.960
Shall we go somewhere
a little bit quieter

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:30.600
and talk in a little bit more detail

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:32.200
and try and get
a better understanding

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:33.560
of how all of this works?

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:35.240
Sure. Let's do it.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:38.000
It's weird to think
how much of our lives

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.280
go on in nondescript server
rooms all over the world.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.000
But what exactly are these
billions of algorithms doing?

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:46.960
So the reason that we use
a list of instructions

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:50.200
is because we have a lot of data
and we have to deal with that data.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:51.520
Now data could be anything.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.120
Data could be a list
of towns in the UK

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.200
and how I get
from one town to another,

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:57.560
or it could be a number of tweets.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:00.240
Which tweet is going to show
up at the top of the list?

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:01.120
Right.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:04.440
Algorithms calculate
based on a bunch of features,

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:07.120
the sort of things that will put
something at the top of the list

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.640
and then something
at the bottom of the list.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.960
So if it's that simple, should
we be scared of algorithms?

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:14.320
Well the trick with algorithms

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.760
that we perhaps should
be a bit concerned about

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:18.640
is what happens in the black box.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.680
So is that like,
when you search for something

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.680
you don't know what their algorithm
is doing because we can't see it.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:24.720
Well a classic case of this

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:27.680
is people talk about
searching for prices for flights,

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:31.120
and depending on which day you search
on you might get a different flight,

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:32.480
where you search from.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.680
And so this can mean
difference of hundreds of pounds.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:37.200
That's an example where
an algorithm is not transparent

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:38.600
and perhaps should be.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:40.960
Can algorithms think for themselves?

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:46.040
Well we wouldn't necessarily
think of a computer as thinking,

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:48.080
but we know that
algorithms can learn.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.480
They can learn
from other algorithms

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.080
and algorithms can create
their own instructions now.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:55.880
But the basis of it
is still the same.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:00.600
Data goes in, goes through
instructions, result comes out.

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:03.080
I'm beginning to get it,

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.840
but I've still never actually
seen an algorithm.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:07.640
I don't even know
what they look like.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:11.200
So I'm heading to one of the UK's
leading coding schools

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:14.160
to see for myself
what goes into making one.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:16.880
{\an2}- So this is code, right?
- Yeah.

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:20.600
So what's the difference
between an algorithm and code?

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:25.880
Coding is algorithms that a computer
can run the instructions for you.

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:28.800
So we have to do it in a language

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:30.560
which the computer
can actually understand.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:32.560
So we've written this in Scratch

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.440
and it's really nice to use,
really intuitive,

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:37.720
and you can just
drag and drop these blocks

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:40.720
and we've got these instructions
for the drone to follow.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:41.760
It's going to do a flip?

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:44.000
It's going to do a flip.
I hope it's going to do a flip.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.040
It's time for a challenge
- a drone race.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:49.720
Izzy's algorithm versus me.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:53.520
So the course is
through the hoop, do a flip,

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.240
come back round,
land and again.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:59.560
So technically, because yours is
programmed by an algorithm,

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.680
you should be able to do
exactly the same thing

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:02.880
three times without a problem.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.640
That's the plan. You ready?
Challenge accepted.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:08.240
{\an2}- Three, two, one.
- I don't know what I'm doing.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:12.800
{\an2}- Take off. Yes.
- Okay.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:13.920
Right.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.040
So we're going set the speed.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:18.400
Fly up. And then it's going fly left.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:20.680
You're already ahead
but I think mine's more reliable.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:24.320
Hopefully it's going go forwards.

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:26.720
Go forwards, there we go.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.000
{\an2}- Nice.
- No, how did it do that?

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:31.440
{\an2}- Yes.
- Full turn.

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:34.360
I'm going to make the
time back in speed.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:37.040
Speed.

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:38.760
{\an2}- And then forwards.
- No!

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:40.760
Go, go, go, go, go, go, go, go, go.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:43.000
{\an2}- Yes, oh, oh, oh!
- Oh. Woah, woah.

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:44.280
Sorry cameraman!

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:47.160
I can just go and make a cup
of tea. I'm just going to leave it.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:50.240
No! [LAUGHS]

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:52.560
And down. Oh look how calm.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.240
Have you done your three laps?
I did do the three laps, yeah.

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:57.840
{\an2}- So you've won?
- Yes.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:02.920
So I think some of the big benefits
of having algorithms versus humans

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:05.760
is that you don't have
the human error

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.080
that, no offence, I think you had.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:11.320
You don't have the human
error in the same way.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:12.840
The computer goes
through the instructions

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:14.400
and that's all they know
how to do.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:17.280
The person writing the code
could have written an error

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:20.280
and that's where problems can arise

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:23.320
but the computer
doesn't make mistakes

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:24.720
it just does what
it's supposed to do.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:28.360
A computer might only do
what it's supposed to do,

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:32.080
but what are the ethical
considerations around algorithms

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:34.880
making so many decisions for us?

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:38.600
One of the public concerns is that
computers are taking over the world,

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:39.920
robots are going
to take over the world,

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.000
algorithms are going
to take all of our jobs.

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:43.200
Is that going to be the case?

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:45.240
Taking our jobs, it's possible.

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:47.520
But also deskilling humans

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:50.560
if we become too dependent upon
them and too trusting of them,

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:51.920
it can deskill us as well.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.200
But on the flip side of it, they can
be hugely beneficial and useful -

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:59.920
speeding up decision-making,
making whole processes efficient,

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:03.000
maybe spotting things that we might
not have spotted ourselves.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:05.280
So we mustn't be frightened of them,

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:07.720
we just must use them
in the correct manner.

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:09.600
What have we learnt then?

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:11.960
Algorithms are actually
remarkably simple.

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:14.560
Just like Bernie said - data goes in,

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:17.760
follows a list of instructions
and a result comes out.

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:19.160
In some parts of the world,

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:22.160
algorithms are now used
in the criminal justice system,

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:24.760
in social care, in credit checks -
they're prolific -

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:29.120
machines making decisions that
directly affect our human lives,

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:32.080
not just the adverts
that you see on the internet

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:34.200
or the people you match
on dating apps.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:36.960
The question for society
isn't the algorithm,

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:39.000
it's who controls the algorithm

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:41.760
and where the data comes from
that goes into them.

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:47.340
Thanks for watching. Don't forget to subscribe

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:50.060
and click the bell to receive notifications for new videos.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:51.880
See you again soon.

